{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Los Angeles Dodgers arrived with championship expectations and quickly showed why, assembling one of the most dominant regular-season runs in modern Dodger history. After a rocky April that saw Kenley Jansen surrender a key hit in a loss, the offense caught fire in late April when Justin Turner and Adrián González delivered clutch hits to spark what would become a historic run. The emergence of rookie Cody Bellinger gave the lineup a new dimension, and by the time June rolled around, the Dodgers were playing the best baseball in baseball — going 21-7 for the month on the strength of grand slams from Chris Taylor and Corey Seager that announced this team's explosive depth.\n\nJuly was nothing short of magical. The Dodgers went 20-3, the best single-month record in the majors that season, powered by Yasiel Puig's towering three-run blast and contributions up and down the roster. Kyle Farmer's pinch-hit double in late July captured the spirit of a team where unlikely heroes kept stepping up. Even as opposing closers occasionally roughed up the bullpen, the wins kept piling up and the division was never in doubt. Los Angeles peaked at one of the best records in the National League and set their sights on October.\n\nAugust brought the first real turbulence. Tony Watson surrendered a Jake Lamb grand slam, Pedro Báez twice gave up costly home runs, and the offense went cold in September — a 12-17 slide that raised uncomfortable questions heading into the postseason. The dominant pitching and thunderous lineup that had carried the team through the summer seemed to flatten out at the worst possible time, and the Dodgers limped into October more uncertain than anyone expected from a 104-win team.\n\nIn the postseason, the Dodgers rose to meet the moment — Justin Turner's NLCS homer helped dispatch the Cubs, and Corey Seager and Enrique Hernández delivered in the World Series against Houston. But the Astros proved a worthy adversary, and the bullpen cracks that appeared in September resurfaced on baseball's biggest stage. Kenley Jansen was touched for a stunning Marwin González tying homer in Game 2, and Altuve's blasts in Games 5 and 7 sealed a devastating Series loss. A brilliant 104-win season ended one step short, leaving Los Angeles with a bittersweet mix of pride and heartbreak.",
  "chapters": [
